  [1mOne problem with the approach first:[0m at the moment [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is called, the client doesn't know the user's ID yet.
  Finding that out is what logging in does. So the caller would have to make up a [38;5;153muserId[39m or look it up separately. Also,
  if the browser supplies the ID, anyone can claim any ID, so it can't be trusted for tracking. Usually it works the
  other way round: the server checks the credentials and sends the [38;5;153muserId[39m back in the login response, and the server
  records the login.

  [1mFirst question:[0m what does "track who logged in" need to do?

